ralph/progress.txt:276:- Security-verification two-test pattern (BE-0344 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/{sql_injection_static_test.go, sql_injection_test.go, AGENTS.md}; structural template for BE-0345..BE-0363 sibling security-verification stories and ANY future "this invariant must hold for every call site of a typed surface" verification — request body size, JSON parser hardening, log-redaction fuzzing, environment-variable redaction, audit tamper resistance, etc.). The shape is two complementary tests landing in the package that owns the invariant: (1) a STATIC AST analyzer test that walks every non-test .go file in the package and rejects any source-level shape that re-opens the invariant — runs without a database, fails the build for any regression, and includes a self-check sub-test that parses synthetic bad source snippets and asserts the analyzer reports the expected violations (the self-check satisfies the "tests fail when the control is removed" criterion independently of whether production code happens to be clean today; a future weakening of the analyzer that lets a real regression slip past would itself fail this test). (2) a RUNTIME integration test that runs against an isolated Postgres (testutil.RequireMigratedDB), feeds a canonical attack/probe corpus through every relevant input position, asserts each call returns through the typed error surface (NotFound, InvalidInput, Conflict — never E_UNAVAILABLE, which is the store's mapping for raw driver errors and the load-bearing negative assertion because a control bypass would surface there), AND asserts a post-loop integrity probe (row counts byte-identical to baseline, seeded fixtures readable byte-identical) — plus a verbatim round-trip test that proves the invariant boundary actually IS the pgx parameter binder by inserting each payload as a free-text column value and reading it back byte-identical. Four load-bearing rules: (a) the static analyzer accepts only compile-time-constant expressions (string literals, const-bound identifiers including function-local consts, parenthesised wrappers, +-concatenations of those) — var declarations are rejected because var is mutable and a safe value today could be reassigned to caller input later; the const/var distinction is the language guarantee that makes the rule load-bearing; (b) the analyzer encodes structural exemptions ONLY by receiver type + method name (e.g. *Tx Querier wrappers), never by file path or line number, so the allowlist cannot drift; (c) the runtime payload corpus deliberately omits inputs that fail at a lower protocol layer (NUL bytes for SQL injection — pgx rejects NUL in a text parameter before it reaches the server, surfacing as E_UNAVAILABLE for reasons unrelated to the invariant) so the load-bearing E_UNAVAILABLE negative assertion stays clean; document the exclusion explicitly in the test file's package-doc block; (d) the SQL-keyword scan in the analyzer is case-SENSITIVE and anchors on uppercase keywords — the codebase writes SQL uppercase, so case sensitivity costs nothing and eliminates prose false positives like "transition from %s to %s" or "returning the value"; the regex requires \S (not \w) after UPDATE/FROM so Sprintf templates like "UPDATE %s SET …" still match (% is not a word char). The package's AGENTS.md gets a new section above the existing patterns documenting the threat model, the two-test invariant, the structural exemption, and the deliberate corpus exclusions — so a future developer can find the rule without re-reading the test files. Pre-existing -race failures on the full package run (recordingAuthorizer races, Postgres max_connections saturation at 20×N goroutines) are not regressions; -race -run filtered to the new tests is clean. NOT applicable to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract with NO static analog (e.g. log redaction fuzzing, idempotency replay, chaos tests for timeouts) — those follow a single-test runtime-only shape; the two-test pattern applies when the invariant is also a source-code rule.
